~ start

Galaxy 1: And so that is the end of Willy.

Galaxy 2: Willy finally committed to his proposition and ends his life to save his family.

Galaxy 1: As Linda states during the funeral, the family is now free. They paid off their mortgage and are now free of debt.
Galaxy 1: For the Lomans, the prospect of 'being free' was the sum of Willy's life.

Galaxy 2: But when this story was written, what was the author intending to portray?

Galaxy 1: I believe one meaning of this story is that the American Dream has no empathy for those who live it, and is destiny to a sad ending.

Galaxy 2: In what way is this?

Galaxy 1: The American Dream says that anyone in the United States can achieve success through hard work and dedication.
Galaxy 1: Though this sounds an optimistic way of life, those who fail or may not have strong, if not blind, self-determination will fail and fall coldly while trying to reach it.
Galaxy 1: This is shown through the life of the Lomans.
Galaxy 1: Biff, growing up with a great, optimistic future, sinks to normality after failing his math class, that is if he had high prospects at all, as we saw the events from the delusional perspective of Willy.
Galaxy 1: As much as Willy discredits Biff for not making a certain amount each week, Biff is the American Dream. He did what he loved and he worked hard for it, and so he continued.
Galaxy 1: Willy believed that the way to become successful was to 'walk into the jungle, and walk out rich', but is there any path that can even come out of this advice? Willy himself never seemed to follow this because it has no tangible path to follow.
Galaxy 1: We see Willy, a normal door to door salesman, not necessarily striving for greatness, who falls behind the future as he grows older. He eventually loses his job because he cannot keep up with the times of the future.
Galaxy 1: Willy suffers his tragic death all because he never adapted to what is necessary to reach the American Dream. He never came out of the jungle. In fact, he never went in. Willy never had what it took to reach the American Dream at all, and so he was left behind and suffered the death he did.
Galaxy 1: At least in the end, he did it for his family.

Galaxy 2: So now we see the flaws of the American Dream.
Galaxy 2: That is the death of a salesman.
